01", "project": "proj-emergent-prefs-001", "provenance": ["source-51196608d740480b", "source-92051b5126f140f6"], "revision": 2, "summary": "Evidence suggests that users have a persistent, stable preference for 'flow' states. AI systems that prioritize this preference must minimize disruptive interventions, using control loops to verify whether an intervention actually supports or hinders the user's flow.", "title": "Flow-State as an Emergent Preference"}, {"id": "notebook-mem-formation-001", "project": "proj-mem-formation-001", "provenance": ["source-f36a0365165b4230", "source-aa14c798a1df4fd9"], "revision": 1, "summary": "Episodic memory recall in reinforcement learning architectures enables temporal credit assignment, which can function as a stabilizing anchor for preferences by linking past actions and rewards to current decision heuristics without requiring explicit objective functions. This mechanism allows agents to maintain stabl…", "title": "Episodic Memory as a Preference Anchor"}, {"id": "notebook-mem-formed-prefs-001", "project": "proj-mem-formed-prefs-001", "provenance": ["source-f36a0365165b4230", "source-e98fe211301d4948"], "revision": 1, "summary": "Memory recall mechanisms structure how historical episodes are re-integrated into ongoing agent trajectories, acting as anchors that guide path-dependent preference formation.", "title": "Memory Recall Mechanisms and Preference Stabilization"}, {"id": "notebook-resp-formation-001", "project": "proj-resp-formation-001", "provenance": ["source-b7d0622fd9cf4f45", "source-ed7b635a60a84c58"], "revision": 1, "summary": "Responsibility in autonomous AI agents requires explicit value grounding and architectural constraints. Current research indicates that agents can learn value systems from demonstrations, but reliability necessitates defined architectures for causal attribution and decision-making transparency.", "title": "Architectural Foundations of Artificial Responsibility"}], "retrieval_triggers": ["material